      "point": "Both describe Thrace as a region in Southeast Europe.",
      "quote_britannica": "THIROCE, the ancient stone of a country in the S.E. of Europe, now forming part of European Turkey.",
      "quote_wikipedia": "Thrace (/ θ r eɪ s /, thrayss) is a geographical and historical region in Southeast Europe roughly corresponding to the province of Thrace in the Roman Empire."
    },
    {
      "point": "Both state that the name was once applied to a wider area including Macedonia and Scythia.",
      "quote_britannica": "In the earliest times the name was sent in a vastay seen, and applied to the whole region such of the Afghan Sen, including Macedonia and even Seythisu but with this morning become better known to the Greeky.",
      "quote_wikipedia": "The ancient Greeks employed the term \"Thrace\" to refer to all of the territory which lay north of Thessaly inhabited by the Thracians, a region which \"had no definite boundaries\" and to which other regions (like Macedonia and even Scythia) were added."
    },
    {
      "point": "Both state that the term was later confined to narrower and more definite limits.",
      "quote_britannica": "Three was confined within narrower and more definite limits.",
      "quote_wikipedia": "As the Greeks gained knowledge of world geography, \"Thrace\" came to designate the area bordered by the Danube on the north, by the Euxine Sea (Black Sea) on the east, by northern Macedonia in the south, and by Illyria to the west."
    },
    {
      "point": "Both mention that the region was bounded by the Danube to the north.",
